#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045498 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/math #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186045622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1007 "Calculus I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1008 "Our Calculus I course provides in-depth coverage of differential calculus including limits, continuity, differentiation techniques, and applications of differentiation. This comprehensive course, together with the following course Calculus II , will prepare students for further studies in advanced mathematics, engineering, statistics, machine learning, and other fields requiring a solid foundation in differential calculus." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1009 "Learn the mathematics of change that underlies science and engineering. Master limits, derivatives, and the basics of integration." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1012 105 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1013 "CA1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046644 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "992cac0c-bf6a-5373-95c9-02edfcfb591e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186064164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1031 "The Candidates Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1033 364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1034 1.5973010157680587 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186048162 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "840e1871-5648-5ed4-855a-86ff3a9aa7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429376 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1031 "Optimizing Distances to Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1033 4218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1034 1.310564925168188 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050536 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "51d6809f-cbef-5f23-be22-3c2d79930c37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186078413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1031 "Graph Transformations of Tangent and Cotangent" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1033 654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050764 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "64c7b4c0-b214-5b04-a40b-5040d931c80a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1031 "Optimizing Distances" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1033 1216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1034 1.447359668376152 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "74e56fe1-9849-55f7-a81d-e8d8802fb0dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198079 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12596" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198079 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ee/eec7d2b67f0b76bb8ec6874cf63f8cb09005f451d684924118983ea1aa39269c.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right)$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x + \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA^2}}=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39ffb646-57fa-5d6d-a81f-286948f66d83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198079 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x, y)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x}\\right).$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}))}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot \\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x})}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{2}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\dfrac{2\\ln x}{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198080 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198080 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points on the curve $y=x^2$ that are closest to the point $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4b55bd03-c8ad-55b2-a51e-7f875d382627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198080 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $\\left( 0, \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)$ and a general point $P(x, y)$:\n\n$$\nS = x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=x^2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nS(x) &=  x^2 + \\left(y- \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2  \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 + \\left( x^2 - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 \\[5pt]\n&=  x^2 +  x^4 -3x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}\\[5pt]\n&=  x^4 - 2 x^2 + \\dfrac{9}{4}.\\\n}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are multiple stationary points, we need to use the candidates test to identify the points of minimum distance. We compute the squared distances for each of the stationary points as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(0) & =(0)^{4}-2(0)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ S(-1) & =(-1)^{4}-2(-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ S(1) & =(1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) is minimized when $x=\\pm 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198083 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198083 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/44ef8fc83ea0a70dcc16769ee6965727ec2c9584e97cc417e34184d633bbf876.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the least distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1$ and the point $(3,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2bf999cc-9733-5f29-90e6-20d297efe352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198083 #edb/ref 1074 "Let us consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(3,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x^2+3}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+3})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+2{x}^{2}+3 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-6x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=6x-6 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =6 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can see that $S''(1) =6 > 0.$ Therefore, $x = 1$ is a minimum of $S.$\n\nSince $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{S(1)}.\n$$\n\nComputing this quantity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3(1)^{2}-6(1)+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186399982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186403011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding an Equation That Determines a Minimum Distance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198246 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "38bfb48e-9b66-58a8-966a-fb6938e08073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Closest Points on a Curve to a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198247 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "cbd4e2eb-d2c8-5447-9a28-4d7da6744712" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186198944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297424 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Smallest Distance From a Point to a Curve" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186198083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198248 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2a7dfacc-de7b-555f-b83a-7b2c6faf69df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186199031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1045 "Optimizing Distances to Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186199027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186198506 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "bd6ad418-e22b-5009-b330-e1172a694c56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199027 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199027 #edb/ref 1061 119264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199027 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25198a50-d410-5851-a1cc-00f60435537f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199027 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199028 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199029 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199028 #edb/ref 1061 119265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199028 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e07168c9-fa86-5a07-b2f0-b9f7aade6c3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199028 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199029 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199029 #edb/ref 1061 119268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199029 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eff95439-12b9-5276-a8f1-b3b4ee7e96f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199029 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199030 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199030 #edb/ref 1061 119269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199030 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "97876c5f-6760-51ef-a427-274559dccac4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199030 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199031 #edb/ref 1061 119272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199031 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "798c8ee2-b4c9-57b8-b340-831e2641810f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199031 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1064 "If the squared distance function has multiple stationary points, we must use the candidates test for *global* extrema to identify which stationary point corresponds to the smallest distance.\n\nTo do that, we evaluate the squared distance at each stationary point and compare the results.\n\nMultiple critical points may correspond to the same squared distance. In such cases, like that shown in the graph below, there would be multiple points of minimum distance.\n\n![](images/de/dea83b2804a816eef505810237940e2d9bb8547ffe77549f833ac04823fa6d30.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1065 7889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c98fb0ba-d8a2-5069-b3c5-04961452caa3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199507 #edb/ref 1067 "The Candidates Test For Multiple Stationary Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use differentiation to find the shortest distance between a point and a curve. \n\nFor example, let's find the smallest distance between the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1$ and the point $(5,1),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc1f5b350025a0fd0530531cfc887c148459194afe209e472701054c128f1079.png)\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1.** Use the distance formula to create a function $S(x,y)$ representing the *squared* distance between $(5,1)$ and a general point $P(x, y){:}$\n\n$$\nS = (x-5)^2 + (y-1)^2\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y=\\sqrt{2x}+1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x}+1-1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2x})^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+25+2x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Calculate the first derivative $S'(x)$ and solve $S'(x) = 0$ to find the stationary point(s).\n\nComputing the derivative $S'(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(x) & =2x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe stationary points of $S(x)$ are the points where $S'(x) = 0.$ Solving this equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{'}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-8 & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Use the first or second derivative tests to confirm that the stationary point minimizes the squared distance.\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\nS''(4) = 2.\n$$\n\nSince $S''(4) > 0,$ the point $x=4$ is a minimum of $S(x)$ by the second derivative test.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the squared distance (and therefore the actual distance) reaches a minimum when $x = 4.$ \n\nFinally, since $S$ gives the squared distance between the two points, the actual distance $d$ between the two points when $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{S(4)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}-8(4)+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1065 7893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ef45dd95-3bca-5734-a692-3cebc9f37432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199508 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{x{log}_{2}⁡2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330520 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4020acf4-04e1-5b60-a2f7-a157b1314764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1080 "{ln}^{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186330987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "731eefef-20d5-574f-9043-1000fa8c1ef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1080 "2x{log}_{2}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f4ee1b1-d9b2-51c6-abc3-dc91b813f3ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331700 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c509c861-23e4-5b8d-8b8d-1044b852afdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186331761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc2baf64-fef0-5fb3-8a66-3f57f615f35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186330987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186331700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "497ee4c0-825b-5385-b6b4-603172d90ac7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/22/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0b5476f-0e78-5366-afe4-a0704f4f9240" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332657 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 4218->654. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429376 #edb/ref 103